The support of a function is the set of points where the function is not zero, or more formally, the closure of the set where the function is non-zero. For the Dirichlet function, which is defined as:

$$
f(x) = \begin{cases} 
0, & \text{if } x \text{ is rational} \\
1, & \text{if } x \text{ is irrational} 
\end{cases}
$$

The set of points where $ f(x) \neq 0 $ is the set of irrational numbers. However, the closure of the set of irrational numbers in the real line is the entire set of real numbers, because the irrationals are dense in $ \mathbb{R} $.

Thus, the support of the Dirichlet function is the entire real line.

$$
\boxed{\mathbb{R}}
$$

